1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,891
Sweetheart, a good beef stew should have

2
00:00:02,891 --> 00:00:04,988
meat so tender you can cut it

3
00:00:04,988 --> 00:00:05,927
with a spoon,

4
00:00:05,927 --> 00:00:08,313
a gravy thick and dark and shiny,

5
00:00:08,313 --> 00:00:10,843
and potatoes that hold their shape.

6
00:00:11,023 --> 00:00:13,724
But most stews I taste today are

7
00:00:13,724 --> 00:00:16,931
gray, watery, and full of tough little

8
00:00:16,931 --> 00:00:19,800
cubes of beef like pencil erasers.

9
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:21,989
And it's not the recipe.

10
00:00:22,169 --> 00:00:25,898
It's five little mistakes that almost everybody

11
00:00:25,898 --> 00:00:28,357
makes, and every one of them is

12
00:00:28,357 --> 00:00:29,309
easy to fix.

13
00:00:29,659 --> 00:00:32,338
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

14
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:33,569
Sunday beef stew,

15
00:00:33,569 --> 00:00:34,510
step by step,

16
00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:36,683
the way she made it every cold

17
00:00:36,683 --> 00:00:38,203
Sunday on Elm Street.

18
00:00:38,383 --> 00:00:39,545
And stay with me,

19
00:00:39,545 --> 00:00:42,276
because the last secret is something she

20
00:00:42,276 --> 00:00:44,189
did in the last ten minutes,

21
00:00:44,189 --> 00:00:46,852
with one ingredient you already have in

22
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:49,311
your kitchen, and it makes the gravy

23
00:00:49,311 --> 00:00:51,770
taste like it simmered for two days.

24
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:53,131
In our house,

25
00:00:53,131 --> 00:00:56,087
beef stew was for the coldest Sundays,

26
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:59,120
after church, when the wind was blowing

27
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,454
snow sideways down Elm Street.

28
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:03,958
My mother started it before we left

29
00:01:03,958 --> 00:01:05,021
for the service,

30
00:01:05,021 --> 00:01:06,747
in a big black iron pot on

31
00:01:06,747 --> 00:01:08,208
the back of the stove,

32
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:09,669
and when we came home,

33
00:01:09,669 --> 00:01:12,060
the whole house smelled so good that

34
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,318
Danny would run up the porch steps

35
00:01:14,318 --> 00:01:16,443
and burn his tongue on the first

36
00:01:16,443 --> 00:01:17,040
spoonful.

37
00:01:17,391 --> 00:01:20,607
Stew was a poor family's best friend,

38
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:21,129
honey.

39
00:01:21,309 --> 00:01:22,816
It took the cheapest,

40
00:01:22,816 --> 00:01:25,471
toughest cut of beef in the butcher's

41
00:01:25,471 --> 00:01:28,270
case and turned it into something you'd

42
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:29,633
serve the minister.

43
00:01:29,813 --> 00:01:31,205
One pound of chuck,

44
00:01:31,205 --> 00:01:32,304
a few potatoes,

45
00:01:32,304 --> 00:01:34,722
a couple of carrots and an onion,

46
00:01:34,722 --> 00:01:37,139
and you could feed six people for

47
00:01:37,139 --> 00:01:40,216
less than a dollar in nineteen fifty-five.

48
00:01:40,396 --> 00:01:42,421
In a minute I'll tell you why

49
00:01:42,421 --> 00:01:44,726
the cheap cut is actually better.

50
00:01:45,077 --> 00:01:47,226
My father ate three bowls,

51
00:01:47,226 --> 00:01:51,276
every time, with my mother's buttermilk biscuits.

52
00:01:51,456 --> 00:01:54,092
And he always said the same thing.

53
00:01:54,272 --> 00:01:57,226
Dorothy, this is better than the last

54
00:01:57,226 --> 00:01:57,546
one.

55
00:01:57,726 --> 00:02:00,106
And my mother always said,

56
00:02:00,106 --> 00:02:02,579
Walter, it's the same stew.

57
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:03,617
And he said,

58
00:02:03,617 --> 00:02:05,475
then I'm getting hungrier.

59
00:02:05,655 --> 00:02:08,797
They had that same conversation every winter

60
00:02:08,797 --> 00:02:10,439
Sunday for forty years.

61
00:02:10,789 --> 00:02:13,563
Here's what you need for six people.

62
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:15,903
Two and a half pounds of beef

63
00:02:15,903 --> 00:02:16,797
chuck roast.

64
00:02:16,977 --> 00:02:19,093
Two tablespoons of flour,

65
00:02:19,093 --> 00:02:20,448
salt and pepper.

66
00:02:20,628 --> 00:02:24,274
Three tablespoons of oil or bacon grease.

67
00:02:24,454 --> 00:02:25,674
One big onion,

68
00:02:25,674 --> 00:02:27,679
three cloves of garlic,

69
00:02:27,679 --> 00:02:30,468
two tablespoons of tomato paste.

70
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:32,784
Four cups of beef broth.

71
00:02:32,964 --> 00:02:35,158
A bay leaf and some thyme.

72
00:02:35,338 --> 00:02:37,562
A pound and a half of small

73
00:02:37,562 --> 00:02:39,456
potatoes, four carrots,

74
00:02:39,456 --> 00:02:41,515
and two stalks of celery.

75
00:02:41,865 --> 00:02:44,210
And here's mistake number one.

76
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:47,304
Buying the little package labeled stew meat.

77
00:02:47,484 --> 00:02:50,300
It's scraps from all different cuts,

78
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,022
some lean, some tough,

79
00:02:52,022 --> 00:02:54,682
and they cook at different speeds,

80
00:02:54,682 --> 00:02:56,872
so half of it's dry and half

81
00:02:56,872 --> 00:02:57,968
of it's chewy.

82
00:02:58,148 --> 00:03:00,321
Buy a whole chuck roast and cut

83
00:03:00,321 --> 00:03:02,285
it yourself into big chunks,

84
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:03,477
about two inches.

85
00:03:03,657 --> 00:03:06,763
Chuck has fat and connective tissue running

86
00:03:06,763 --> 00:03:09,147
through it, and after a long slow

87
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:11,459
cook, that melts into the gravy.

88
00:03:11,809 --> 00:03:13,817
Big chunks, honey.

89
00:03:13,997 --> 00:03:15,646
Not little ones.

90
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:18,295
Little cubes dry out before they get

91
00:03:18,295 --> 00:03:18,775
tender.

92
00:03:18,955 --> 00:03:21,718
Two-inch chunks stay juicy inside.

93
00:03:21,898 --> 00:03:24,148
My mother cut them so big that

94
00:03:24,148 --> 00:03:26,697
Danny once asked if we were having

95
00:03:26,697 --> 00:03:27,447
pot roast.

96
00:03:27,627 --> 00:03:30,105
She said, it's stew if it's in

97
00:03:30,105 --> 00:03:32,665
gravy, and pot roast if it's on

98
00:03:32,665 --> 00:03:33,490
a platter.

99
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:34,425
Same meat.

100
00:03:34,775 --> 00:03:37,410
Pat the beef dry with paper towels.

101
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:39,064
That's important.

102
00:03:39,244 --> 00:03:41,882
Then season it with salt and pepper,

103
00:03:41,882 --> 00:03:43,714
and toss it in the flour.

104
00:03:43,894 --> 00:03:45,873
Heat the oil in a heavy pot

105
00:03:45,873 --> 00:03:48,805
over medium-high heat until it shimmers.

106
00:03:48,985 --> 00:03:51,272
And here's mistake number two.

107
00:03:51,452 --> 00:03:52,741
Crowding the pot.

108
00:03:52,921 --> 00:03:54,944
If you dump all the meat in

109
00:03:54,944 --> 00:03:57,865
at once, it steams instead of browning,

110
00:03:57,865 --> 00:03:59,214
and it turns gray.

111
00:03:59,394 --> 00:04:01,394
Brown it in three batches,

112
00:04:01,394 --> 00:04:03,701
with space between the pieces.

113
00:04:04,051 --> 00:04:06,670
Leave each piece alone until it has

114
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:09,139
a dark brown crust on the bottom,

115
00:04:09,139 --> 00:04:10,636
about three minutes,

116
00:04:10,636 --> 00:04:11,609
then turn it.

117
00:04:11,789 --> 00:04:13,426
Don't keep poking at it.

118
00:04:13,606 --> 00:04:15,921
That dark crust is where all the

119
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:17,948
flavor of a stew comes from.

120
00:04:18,128 --> 00:04:20,224
Put each batch on a plate as

121
00:04:20,224 --> 00:04:20,972
it's done.

122
00:04:21,152 --> 00:04:23,490
And don't you dare wash out that

123
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:24,658
pot, sweetheart.

124
00:04:24,838 --> 00:04:27,244
Those brown bits stuck to the bottom

125
00:04:27,244 --> 00:04:27,845
are gold.

126
00:04:28,196 --> 00:04:29,922
If this is helping you,

127
00:04:29,922 --> 00:04:31,948
tap the like button for me,

128
00:04:31,948 --> 00:04:32,398
honey.

129
00:04:32,578 --> 00:04:35,516
It helps Grandma more than you know.

130
00:04:35,696 --> 00:04:37,979
Now turn the heat down to medium,

131
00:04:37,979 --> 00:04:40,193
and add the chopped onion to the

132
00:04:40,193 --> 00:04:40,816
same pot.

133
00:04:40,996 --> 00:04:42,229
Stir it around,

134
00:04:42,229 --> 00:04:44,615
scraping up those brown bits,

135
00:04:44,615 --> 00:04:46,506
for about five minutes,

136
00:04:46,506 --> 00:04:47,822
until it's soft.

137
00:04:48,002 --> 00:04:50,387
Add the garlic and the tomato paste,

138
00:04:50,387 --> 00:04:52,574
and cook it for two more minutes,

139
00:04:52,574 --> 00:04:54,959
until the paste turns a darker brick

140
00:04:54,959 --> 00:04:55,224
red.

141
00:04:55,574 --> 00:04:57,480
Pour in a splash of the broth

142
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,584
and scrape the bottom of the pot

143
00:04:59,584 --> 00:05:02,017
with a wooden spoon until it's clean.

144
00:05:02,197 --> 00:05:04,390
Then add the rest of the broth,

145
00:05:04,390 --> 00:05:06,301
the bay leaf and the thyme,

146
00:05:06,301 --> 00:05:08,069
and put the beef back in,

147
00:05:08,069 --> 00:05:10,475
with all the juice from the plate.

148
00:05:10,655 --> 00:05:12,474
Bring it just to a boil,

149
00:05:12,474 --> 00:05:14,521
then turn it down as low as

150
00:05:14,521 --> 00:05:15,355
it will go,

151
00:05:15,355 --> 00:05:16,492
put the lid on,

152
00:05:16,492 --> 00:05:18,387
and let it barely bubble.

153
00:05:18,738 --> 00:05:20,978
And mistake number three.

154
00:05:21,158 --> 00:05:22,621
Boiling it hard.

155
00:05:22,801 --> 00:05:25,054
A hard boil makes the meat seize

156
00:05:25,054 --> 00:05:27,166
up and turn stringy and tough,

157
00:05:27,166 --> 00:05:29,349
no matter how long you cook it.

158
00:05:29,529 --> 00:05:31,791
You want a lazy little bubble,

159
00:05:31,791 --> 00:05:34,730
just breaking the surface now and then.

160
00:05:34,910 --> 00:05:36,855
Or put the covered pot in a

161
00:05:36,855 --> 00:05:39,593
three hundred twenty-five degree oven,

162
00:05:39,593 --> 00:05:41,249
which is what I do now.

163
00:05:41,429 --> 00:05:43,872
It cooks gently on all sides,

164
00:05:43,872 --> 00:05:46,399
and you can't burn the bottom.

165
00:05:46,749 --> 00:05:49,052
Let it go for an hour and

166
00:05:49,052 --> 00:05:49,697
a half.

167
00:05:49,877 --> 00:05:52,490
Then comes mistake number four.

168
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:55,201
Putting the vegetables in at the beginning.

169
00:05:55,381 --> 00:05:57,915
If the potatoes and carrots cook for

170
00:05:57,915 --> 00:06:00,308
three hours, they turn to mush and

171
00:06:00,308 --> 00:06:02,068
disappear into the gravy.

172
00:06:02,248 --> 00:06:04,425
Add them after an hour and a

173
00:06:04,425 --> 00:06:06,446
half, cut into big pieces,

174
00:06:06,446 --> 00:06:08,389
and give it another hour,

175
00:06:08,389 --> 00:06:11,188
until a fork slides through the beef

176
00:06:11,188 --> 00:06:13,754
and the potatoes are just tender.

177
00:06:14,104 --> 00:06:15,984
How do you know the beef is

178
00:06:15,984 --> 00:06:16,333
done?

179
00:06:16,513 --> 00:06:18,770
Take out one piece and press it

180
00:06:18,770 --> 00:06:19,717
with a spoon.

181
00:06:19,897 --> 00:06:21,634
If it falls apart,

182
00:06:21,634 --> 00:06:22,695
it's ready.

183
00:06:22,875 --> 00:06:24,352
If it fights back,

184
00:06:24,352 --> 00:06:26,486
give it another half hour.

185
00:06:26,666 --> 00:06:28,531
It's not a matter of time,

186
00:06:28,531 --> 00:06:31,043
honey, it's a matter of tenderness.

187
00:06:31,223 --> 00:06:33,635
Every piece of beef is different,

188
00:06:33,635 --> 00:06:35,827
and the pot will tell you when

189
00:06:35,827 --> 00:06:37,655
it's done, if you listen.

190
00:06:38,005 --> 00:06:40,033
Now the secret I promised you,

191
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:41,453
the last ten minutes.

192
00:06:41,633 --> 00:06:44,080
Mistake number five is a flat,

193
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:47,262
dull gravy that tastes like nothing but

194
00:06:47,262 --> 00:06:47,670
salt.

195
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:50,601
My mother fixed it with one ingredient.

196
00:06:50,781 --> 00:06:52,488
A splash of vinegar.

197
00:06:52,668 --> 00:06:55,826
One tablespoon of plain cider vinegar or

198
00:06:55,826 --> 00:06:57,168
red wine vinegar,

199
00:06:57,168 --> 00:06:59,379
stirred in right at the end.

200
00:06:59,559 --> 00:07:01,985
Taste it before and after.

201
00:07:02,165 --> 00:07:04,212
It wakes up the whole pot like

202
00:07:04,212 --> 00:07:05,509
turning on a light.

203
00:07:05,859 --> 00:07:08,650
Vinegar doesn't make it taste sour,

204
00:07:08,650 --> 00:07:09,527
sweetheart.

205
00:07:09,707 --> 00:07:12,304
It cuts through the richness and makes

206
00:07:12,304 --> 00:07:14,763
the beef taste beefier and the gravy

207
00:07:14,763 --> 00:07:15,652
taste deeper.

208
00:07:15,832 --> 00:07:18,152
Good chefs do the same thing with

209
00:07:18,152 --> 00:07:19,489
a squeeze of lemon.

210
00:07:19,669 --> 00:07:21,794
And if your gravy is too thin,

211
00:07:21,794 --> 00:07:24,133
mash two or three of the potatoes

212
00:07:24,133 --> 00:07:26,329
against the side of the pot and

213
00:07:26,329 --> 00:07:27,250
stir them in.

214
00:07:27,430 --> 00:07:29,543
They thicken it naturally,

215
00:07:29,543 --> 00:07:31,168
with no lumpy flour.

216
00:07:31,519 --> 00:07:33,636
And finish it with a handful of

217
00:07:33,636 --> 00:07:36,573
frozen peas and some chopped fresh parsley,

218
00:07:36,573 --> 00:07:39,100
stirred in right before you serve it.

219
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,637
The peas only need two minutes in

220
00:07:41,637 --> 00:07:42,565
the hot stew.

221
00:07:42,745 --> 00:07:44,965
They add a little sweetness and a

222
00:07:44,965 --> 00:07:46,310
pop of bright green,

223
00:07:46,310 --> 00:07:48,597
and the parsley makes it look like

224
00:07:48,597 --> 00:07:50,211
a picture in a magazine.

225
00:07:50,391 --> 00:07:52,364
My mother grew her parsley in a

226
00:07:52,364 --> 00:07:54,910
coffee can on the kitchen windowsill all

227
00:07:54,910 --> 00:07:55,673
winter long.

228
00:07:56,024 --> 00:07:58,338
And if you want a little extra

229
00:07:58,338 --> 00:08:01,654
flavor, honey, here are my grown-up tricks.

230
00:08:01,834 --> 00:08:04,269
Replace one cup of the broth with

231
00:08:04,269 --> 00:08:05,891
a cup of red wine or a

232
00:08:05,891 --> 00:08:08,547
dark beer, poured in when you scrape

233
00:08:08,547 --> 00:08:09,137
the pot.

234
00:08:09,317 --> 00:08:12,122
Or add a spoonful of Worcestershire sauce

235
00:08:12,122 --> 00:08:13,148
with the broth.

236
00:08:13,328 --> 00:08:16,127
My husband liked a few mushrooms browned

237
00:08:16,127 --> 00:08:18,296
in butter and stirred in at the

238
00:08:18,296 --> 00:08:18,576
end.

239
00:08:18,756 --> 00:08:20,191
Whatever you add,

240
00:08:20,191 --> 00:08:21,964
add it one at a time,

241
00:08:21,964 --> 00:08:22,808
and taste.

242
00:08:23,158 --> 00:08:24,784
Serve it in deep bowls,

243
00:08:24,784 --> 00:08:27,684
with buttermilk biscuits or a thick slice

244
00:08:27,684 --> 00:08:30,089
of bread for mopping up the gravy.

245
00:08:30,269 --> 00:08:33,106
And here's the truth about beef stew,

246
00:08:33,106 --> 00:08:33,566
honey.

247
00:08:33,746 --> 00:08:36,080
It's even better the next day.

248
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:37,239
Let it cool,

249
00:08:37,239 --> 00:08:39,770
put it in the icebox overnight,

250
00:08:39,770 --> 00:08:41,565
and the flavors marry.

251
00:08:41,745 --> 00:08:43,987
You can lift the hardened fat off

252
00:08:43,987 --> 00:08:45,549
the top in the morning,

253
00:08:45,549 --> 00:08:45,820
too.

254
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,468
I always make it on Saturday for

255
00:08:48,468 --> 00:08:49,007
Sunday.

256
00:08:49,358 --> 00:08:52,408
It freezes beautifully for three months,

257
00:08:52,408 --> 00:08:53,247
sweetheart.

258
00:08:53,427 --> 00:08:55,538
Freeze it in quart containers,

259
00:08:55,538 --> 00:08:57,227
and on a busy weeknight,

260
00:08:57,227 --> 00:08:59,760
you've got a dinner that tastes like

261
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,449
you spent all afternoon.

262
00:09:01,629 --> 00:09:04,260
The potatoes get a little soft when

263
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:06,890
frozen, so if I'm freezing a batch,

264
00:09:06,890 --> 00:09:08,919
I leave them out and boil a

265
00:09:08,919 --> 00:09:11,098
few fresh ones when I warm it

266
00:09:11,098 --> 00:09:11,324
up.

267
00:09:11,674 --> 00:09:13,229
So one more time.

268
00:09:13,409 --> 00:09:15,997
Buy a whole chuck roast and cut

269
00:09:15,997 --> 00:09:16,916
big chunks.

270
00:09:17,096 --> 00:09:18,686
Brown it in batches,

271
00:09:18,686 --> 00:09:20,753
and keep those brown bits.

272
00:09:20,933 --> 00:09:23,477
Cook the onion and tomato paste in

273
00:09:23,477 --> 00:09:24,450
the same pot.

274
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:26,298
Never a hard boil,

275
00:09:26,298 --> 00:09:28,800
just a lazy bubble or a low

276
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:29,263
oven.

277
00:09:29,443 --> 00:09:31,730
Put the vegetables in halfway.

278
00:09:31,910 --> 00:09:34,354
And finish it with a splash of

279
00:09:34,354 --> 00:09:36,798
vinegar and a handful of peas.

280
00:09:36,978 --> 00:09:39,378
That's beef stew the way my mother

281
00:09:39,378 --> 00:09:41,778
made it, and nobody's ever done it

282
00:09:41,778 --> 00:09:42,272
better.

283
00:09:42,622 --> 00:09:44,588
Tell me in the comments,

284
00:09:44,588 --> 00:09:45,489
sweetheart.

